sentences.l:
And there was a certain Euchenor, son of Polyidus the seer,
rich and good, at Corinth having his home,
who, knowing well a deadly death-fate, went aboard a ship;

for often to him had told the old man, good Polyidus,
by a grievous sickness to perish in his own halls,
or among the Achaeans’ ships by the Trojans to be subdued;

and soon even the glory of the Achaeans
would have been; for such an earth-holder, earth-shaker
was urging on the Argives, and besides with might himself he defended;

And there the Boeotians and the trailing-tunicked Ionians,
Locrians and Phthians and the shining Epeans,
with effort held him rushing on from the ships, and were not able
to thrust from themselves, like flame, heavenly Hector;
these, of the Athenians, picked men;

and among them
led the son of Peteos, Menestheus, and along with him followed
Pheidas and Stichius and Bias, the good;

but he dwelt
in Phylace, away from his fatherland, having killed a man,
a kinsman of his stepmother Eriopis, whom Oileus had;

and around them
at the roots of the horns much sweat wells up;
the two, indeed, the yoke alone, well-polished, holds apart
as they press along the furrow; and it cuts the boundary of the field;

but with bows and with well-twisted sheep’s wool
to Ilios together they followed, trusting, with which then
often casting they broke the battle-lines of the Trojans;
